WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.520
Vision transformers are overtaking convolutional networks,

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.185
but their size makes privacy-preserving distributed
training hard.

00:00:09.265 --> 00:00:12.153
Federated learning must move whole models,

00:00:12.233 --> 00:00:19.221
and split learning leaks privacy because a
transformer's smashed data still resembles the raw input.

00:00:19.301 --> 00:00:23.109
From Yonsei, Deakin, M.I.T., and Oulu, at NeurIPS 2022,

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:25.371
this paper proposes DP-CutMixSL,

00:00:25.451 --> 00:00:31.449
pairing a Gaussian differential-privacy mechanism
with a patch-level randomized CutMix

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:34.347
that mixes masked patches across clients.

00:00:34.427 --> 00:00:37.740
It strengthens privacy while improving accuracy.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.577
We want to train on user data without exposing it.

00:00:41.657 --> 00:00:46.034
Federated learning keeps data local but exchanges
whole models,

00:00:46.114 --> 00:00:48.156
costly for large transformers.

00:00:48.236 --> 00:00:53.462
Split learning instead shares only cut-layer
activations, the smashed data.

00:00:53.542 --> 00:00:56.858
But transformers have no pooling or convolution,

00:00:56.938 --> 00:01:03.366
so their smashed data barely distorts the input and
stays visually similar to the raw image.

00:01:03.446 --> 00:01:08.035
That similarity leaks privacy and, preserving so
much information,

00:01:08.115 --> 00:01:10.370
also inflates communication cost.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:15.507
As transformers displace CNNs, do the old privacy
recipes still hold?

00:01:15.587 --> 00:01:18.311
The authors note three things about ViT.

00:01:18.391 --> 00:01:23.148
First, without pooling its hidden representation is
barely distorted,

00:01:23.228 --> 00:01:27.283
so regularizing it works as well as regularizing
the input,

00:01:27.363 --> 00:01:29.667
yet high mutual information leaks.

00:01:29.747 --> 00:01:35.485
Second, global self-attention makes ViT robust to
large noise on part of the image,

00:01:35.565 --> 00:01:37.448
ideal for Cutout and CutMix.

00:01:37.528 --> 00:01:40.112
Third, every operation is patch-level.

00:01:40.192 --> 00:01:46.070
Together these point to a patch-level randomized
CutMix of the hidden representation.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.589
Here is DP-CutMixSL step by step.

00:01:48.669 --> 00:01:56.256
A mixer draws mixing ratios lambda from a Dirichlet
distribution and builds a pseudorandom binary mask per client,

00:01:56.336 --> 00:01:59.821
with on-patches proportional to that client's lambda.

00:01:59.901 --> 00:02:04.596
Each client runs its input through the lower model
to get smashed data,

00:02:04.676 --> 00:02:06.883
masks it into Cutout smashed data,

00:02:06.963 --> 00:02:11.860
then adds white Gaussian noise for DP-Cutout
smashed data, and uploads it.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:17.778
Because the masks are mutually exclusive and
collectively exhaustive at the patch level,

00:02:17.858 --> 00:02:24.503
the server simply adds the clients' noisy patches
into DP-CutMix smashed data with no blank patches,

00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:27.261
mixing labels by the same lambda weights.

00:02:27.341 --> 00:02:32.305
Forward and backward propagation then proceed as in
vanilla split learning.

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:37.080
Crucially, only a noisy fraction of each client's
data is ever exposed.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:43.524
Without noise, CutMixSL gives the best top-1
accuracy in every configuration but one.

00:02:43.604 --> 00:02:49.089
On CIFAR-10 with ViT-Tiny it reaches seventy-three
point seven seven percent,

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:54.365
versus fifty-seven for plain split learning and
sixty-eight for SplitFed.

00:02:54.445 --> 00:02:57.835
With PiT-Tiny it hits seventy-one point two six.

00:02:57.915 --> 00:03:03.328
On Fashion-MNIST it tops eighty-nine percent across
all three architectures.

00:03:03.408 --> 00:03:08.893
The exception is VGG-16, where Mixup edges ahead,
because CNNs focus locally,

00:03:08.973 --> 00:03:14.820
so replacing whole patches costs them more
information than it costs transformers.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.113
Ablations probe the privacy-accuracy trade-off.

00:03:19.193 --> 00:03:23.105
Sweeping noise variance,

00:03:23.185 --> 00:03:27.098
DP-CutMixSL has the best accuracy at nearly every
level and always beats DP-MixSL.

00:03:27.178 --> 00:03:31.091
Its RDP epsilon is tighter than DP-SL but looser
than DP-MixSL,

00:03:31.171 --> 00:03:35.084
exactly the trade-off theory predicts,

00:03:35.164 --> 00:03:39.076
since Mixup melts information across the whole
representation.

00:03:39.156 --> 00:03:43.069
Larger mixing groups lower both accuracy and
epsilon, a hiding-in-the-crowd effect.

00:03:43.149 --> 00:03:47.062
A reconstruction attack shows robustness rising
from raw data to Mixup, patch CutMix,

00:03:47.142 --> 00:03:51.055
then Cutout.

00:03:51.135 --> 00:03:55.047
Finally, accuracy grows as clients scale from two to ten,

00:03:55.127 --> 00:03:59.040
and SplitFed-style averaging helps further.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.839
The takeaway: for vision transformers,

00:04:01.919 --> 00:04:05.653
you need not trade accuracy for privacy in split learning.

00:04:05.733 --> 00:04:11.243
Adding Gaussian noise and mixing randomly masked
patches across clients amplifies the

00:04:11.323 --> 00:04:16.306
differential-privacy guarantee over plain split
learning and raises accuracy,

00:04:16.386 --> 00:04:19.660
with the gain provably bounded by a Mixup baseline.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.592
It works because transformers use global
self-attention at the patch level,

00:04:24.672 --> 00:04:28.604
so swapping patches costs little, though it would
hurt a CNN.

00:04:28.684 --> 00:04:33.470
Patch-level CutMix is a privacy regularizer built
for the transformer era.
